ures to  deal with bias training after a summer  of black lives matter protests  senate bill 108 would require any person  employed in the criminal justice system  to take implicit  bias and cultural competency training  it's sponsored by the senate judiciary  committee  a bill requiring police officers to  complete those trainings already took  effect back in 2019  this bill would apply to other people in  law enforcement mainly  the courts and attorneys it does not  have a hearing schedule just yet  democrats are making another run at  passing a dignity in  death bill it's the fourth straight  session a physician assisted suicide  bill has been  introduced the 2019 version passed the  senate but not the assembly  this version is sponsored by state  senator dallas harris  in this case a secondary physician and  two witnesses would have to sign off on  administering the lethal drugs  oregon was the first state to pass a  dignity and death measure about 20 years  ago  so far there about a thousand people  have taken advantage  more than 90 percent of them were in  hospice care at the time  another big topic is economic assistance  more than half a billion dollars in  federal money is being set aside for  renters and schools here in southern  nevada  the spending was approved by the interim  finance committee this week  in all 208 million dollars is going to  rental assistance programs  nevada's division of housing estimates  it'll help about 20 thousand households  162 million dollars will be sent spent  here in clark county money will go  directly from the state's account  right to the landlord the clark county  school district is getting a larger  chunk of money as well about 350 million  dollars  but it's important to note this will be  on top of money that's already in the  distributive school account  the money is slated to help reopen  schools safely and outfit them with new  technology state senate majority leader  nicole cannizzaro told 8 news now some  ways 